course: dictionary
lesson: 11965
title: Catastrophically
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Catastrophically is an adverb. It tells us how something happened. It means in a way that causes terrible damage or disaster. «The flood spread catastrophically across the valley, washing away bridges and homes.» The words washing away bridges and homes are the clues: the damage is huge.
explain: We also use catastrophically in everyday talk to make a failure sound extra bad. «Our cake failed catastrophically: it sank in the middle and burned at the edges.» The cake was not really a disaster, but the writer wants you to know it went very, very wrong.
explain: A good check is to swap in a simpler word. In «The plan went catastrophically wrong», terribly fits, so you know catastrophically means very badly. If the swap makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Catastrophically means in a way that causes terrible damage or disaster.
fact: Catastrophe is a word for a great disaster, and catastrophically describes how something happens.
fact: «Catastrophically» is used in news and geography to describe floods, earthquakes and failures.
fact: In everyday talk, «catastrophically» can exaggerate how badly something went, as in «the game failed catastrophically».
fact: Phrases to remember: «went catastrophically wrong» and «failed catastrophically».
exercise: «The town was hit by a flood, and the roads and bridges were damaged catastrophically.» What does catastrophically mean here? | In a slow and calm way | In a way that is very quiet | *In a way that causes terrible damage | In a way that makes everybody laugh
exercise: «The cake failed catastrophically in the oven.» What does this tell the reader? | The cake had too much sugar in it | *The cake was badly ruined, far worse than a small mistake | The cake was bigger than usual | The cake was eaten very quickly
exercise: Which word could replace catastrophically in «The plan went catastrophically wrong»? | slowly | happily | quietly | *terribly
test: Which sentence uses catastrophically to show a real disaster? | *«The earthquake shook the city catastrophically, and many buildings fell.» | «We ate our lunch catastrophically at noon.» | «The sun rose catastrophically at six o'clock.» | «The puppy slept catastrophically quietly on the rug.»
test: Which clue in «The river flooded catastrophically, drowning the fields» shows the damage was huge? | the river | flooded | *drowning the fields | the
exam: Which word means in a way that causes terrible damage or disaster? | *catastrophically | happily | gently | quickly
